Tranche 2 Deliverable 3: the MoneyGram Access sandbox, how far the client gets
and where exactly it stops.

The criterion names "the MoneyGram Access sandbox" and then names the SDF
Stellar Test Anchor as covering the same flows "during MoneyGram onboarding".
This is what happens when the same client is pointed at MoneyGram directly,
with no onboarding done, so that the onboarding gate is a demonstrated fact
rather than an assertion.

  anchor      extmgxanchor.moneygram.com        (testnet)
  account     GC35EAQJPYWZDPOUMOOLGETVTDMG3KQDKOX4ISGOLXS2RR4JLLLGBFF2
  client      anchor/sep_client.py, unchanged, --home-domain is the only difference

== SEP-1, their toml

  SIGNING_KEY               GCUZ6YLL5RQBTYLTTQLPCM73C5XAIUGK2TIMWQH7HPSGWVS2KJ2F3CHS
  WEB_AUTH_ENDPOINT         https://extmgxanchor.moneygram.com/stellarsepservice/auth
  TRANSFER_SERVER_SEP0024   https://extmgxanchor.moneygram.com/stellarsepservice/sep24
  currency USDC             GBBD47IF6LWK7P7MDEVSCWR7DPUWV3NY3DTQEVFL4NAT4AQH3ZLLFLA5

  That issuer is this deployment's USDC. The SAC of USDC:GBBD47IF is
  CBIELTK6YBZJU5UP2WWQEUCYKLPU6AUNZ2BQ4WWFEIE3USCIHMXQDAMA, which is
  contracts.usdc here. So a MoneyGram sandbox deposit would land as exactly the
  asset the Vault custodies, with no swap in the middle.

== SEP-10, their auth

  WORKS. Challenge fetched, verified against their SIGNING_KEY, signed, and a
  JWT returned:

    sub          GC35EAQJPYWZDPOUMOOLGETVTDMG3KQDKOX4ISGOLXS2RR4JLLLGBFF2
    home_domain  extmgxanchor.moneygram.com
    exp          +24h

  No onboarding was needed for this. Their older host,
  extstellar.moneygram.com, refuses the same call with HTTP 400, which is the
  registration gate on the legacy path; the newer one does not gate SEP-10.

== SEP-24, their limits and their transaction

  /info      deposit  USDC enabled, min 15, max 950
             withdraw USDC enabled, min 15, max 2500

  A 5 USDC deposit is refused for being under their minimum, which is their
  business rule answering correctly. At 20 USDC:

    transaction   c18316d7-9626-4365-a34c-35af9b95ecaa
    type          interactive_customer_info_needed
    url           https://extramps.moneygram.com?transaction_id=...&token=...

== Where it stops

  The interactive URL loads the MoneyGram Ramps widget and fails:

    Something went wrong
    We couldn't load the information needed to continue.
    Error code: RMP-01
    Transaction ID: c18316d7-9626-4365-a34c-35af9b95ecaa

  The widget's own URL carries `wallet_provider=` empty. That is the gate: the
  partner is identified to the widget by a registered wallet provider, and this
  one is not registered, so the widget cannot resolve the configuration it needs
  and stops before collecting anything.

== What this means for the deliverable

  Everything the integration owns works against MoneyGram itself: their toml,
  their SEP-10, their SEP-24, their limits, their transaction. What does not
  work is the part that is not code, which is being a registered MoneyGram
  partner, and it fails with their own error code at their own widget.

  That is the onboarding the award refers to when it names the SDF Stellar Test
  Anchor as covering these flows during it. The Test Anchor round trip is in
  docs/evidence/anchor-roundtrip-2026-10-30.log and is closed end to end.

== Addendum, same evening: client domain attestation

The RMP-01 above had a cause, and it was ours rather than theirs.

MoneyGram identifies the wallet provider driving a SEP-10 session through
`client_domain`. Asked for one, their auth endpoint answered precisely:

  GET .../auth?account=GC35EAQJ...&client_domain=agama.finance
  400 {"error":"Unable to read client_domain's SIGNING_KEY"}

Because https://agama.finance/.well-known/stellar.toml was a 404. The mechanism
is attestation and not a claim: the client names its domain, the anchor fetches
that file, reads SIGNING_KEY, adds a second ManageData operation naming the
domain, and requires the challenge to carry that key's signature as well as the
account's. A client asserting a domain it does not control cannot produce it.

So the file now exists, served from prod/public/.well-known and copied
explicitly by prod/deploy.sh because a dot-directory is what an exporter drops
quietly:

  https://agama.finance/.well-known/stellar.toml      HTTP 200
  SIGNING_KEY   GCGRQ2LCB4RJ65XNKKIWGHLK3TDHPM7LNKX5DN4SRJH6K5NGQTS6YOSG
  ORG_NAME      Agama Labs Inc.
  ORG_DBA       Agama Finance

That key signs SEP-10 challenges and nothing else. It holds no balance, issues
no asset, and is admin of no contract.

anchor/sep_client.py takes --client-domain and --client-domain-key, and it
verifies before it co-signs: the challenge has to carry a client_domain
operation and that operation has to name the domain we asked for. An anchor
that quietly dropped it would otherwise have us spending the domain signature
on a session that does not carry the domain.

With that in place, against the same anchor:

  SEP-10    JWT received, and it now carries   client_domain: agama.finance
  SEP-24    transaction 58622f3f-b0d5-41de-839c-3bb4fe656c2f
  widget    renders the calculator, "Finding nearby locations...", then
            Error code: RMP-01, RMP-02

So the attestation moved it forward: the widget got far enough to try, where
before it failed immediately. What remains is the partner whitelist, and the
ask is now exact rather than general: whitelist agama.finance as a wallet
provider on the Stellar SEP path, not the widget path.

== Addendum 2: what RMP-01 and RMP-02 actually are

Instrumented the widget rather than reading its error text. With the client
domain attested, two of MoneyGram's own backend calls fail, and both fail with
a server error rather than a refusal:

  POST https://extramps.moneygram.com/rampsservice/v3/transactions/prepare      500
  GET  https://extramps.moneygram.com/rampsservice/v3/reference-data/countries  500

Everything else the page loads returns 200: fifty-odd asset requests, their
Sentry and PostHog beacons, the US flag. Only those two.

`transactions/prepare` is where the widget would resolve the partner
configuration for the session, and `reference-data/countries` is what drives
"Finding nearby locations...". Both return 500 for a session whose
`client_domain` is attested and whose wallet provider is not registered.

Worth saying precisely, because it is the content of the support request: their
sandbox does not answer 401 or 403 for an unregistered partner, it answers 500.
From outside the app those paths fall through to the SPA's index.html, so they
cannot be probed directly; the status codes above are from the widget's own
session.

== What is left, and what it is not

Not code. The integration drives their anchor end to end: SEP-1, SEP-10 with
client domain attestation, SEP-24 with their limits enforced and a transaction
opened. The two calls that fail are theirs, on their host, for a partner they
have not registered.

The request to make is therefore narrow: whitelist agama.finance as a wallet
provider on the Stellar SEP path, not the widget path, and the evidence that it
is worth doing is that everything up to their own 500 already works.

  client domain     agama.finance
  SIGNING_KEY       GCGRQ2LCB4RJ65XNKKIWGHLK3TDHPM7LNKX5DN4SRJH6K5NGQTS6YOSG
  toml              https://agama.finance/.well-known/stellar.toml   HTTP 200
  account           GC35EAQJPYWZDPOUMOOLGETVTDMG3KQDKOX4ISGOLXS2RR4JLLLGBFF2
  last transaction  4ddb0b16-b40a-41f7-8ce6-6698f7c0dead
